  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Humanitarian, Protection and Health Needs of Undocumented Returnees at the Borders and Longer-Term Needs of Returnees and IDPs in Key Provinces of High Return</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">More than 1.9 million Afghans returned to the country since 2023, including more than 665,300 from Pakistan as of 13 July 2024 since 15 September 2023. Many returnees are arriving in areas already facing protracted displacement. Over the twelve-month duration of this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) estimates that 1,398,060 undocumented returnees will cross into Afghanistan through IOM reception centres at the Milak and Islam Qala border points on the border with Iran and Spin Boldak and Torkham on the border with Pakistan. The objective of the project is to enhance the resilience of vulnerable Afghan populations by addressing their immediate humanitarian needs at the border areas and strengthening durable solutions pathways in the areas of return, through a comprehensive, evidence-informed and data-driven response. IOM is utilizing an area-based, integrated approach and achieving the project&#x2019;s objective through a multi-sectoral intervention at border areas and provinces of high return. To complement this, IOM&#x2019;s Displacement Tracking Matrix programmes continues to contribute to humanitarian and development partners&#x2019; ability to make informed and evidence-based decisions.</narrative>
  </description>
